        "date":  "2026-03-25",
        "theme":  "Enterprise",
        "summary":  "The article examines why AI projects frequently fail due to organizational rather than technical issues and provides guidance on when to salvage initiatives versus when to shut them down. Success requires clear business alignment, proper workflow orchestration, transparent communication, and outcomes tied to business value rather than model performance alone.",
        "execSummary":  "Most failing AI initiatives are organizational failures masquerading as technical ones ΓÇö a distinction that matters because the wrong diagnosis leads to the wrong remedy, such as swapping models instead of fixing process alignment. The article\u0027s triage framework is useful for enterprise leaders managing portfolios of AI pilots: assess whether the initiative has measurable business alignment, clear ownership, and integrated workflows before investing in remediation. For customer-support leaders with stalled AI projects, the biggest signal to watch is whether the initiative is measuring AI behavior or business outcomes ΓÇö if it\u0027s the former only, the project is at high risk. The decision to cut versus rescue should be made on business-value trajectory, not sunk cost.",
