        "date":  "2026-08-27",
        "theme":  "Agents",
        "summary":  "Large enterprises are adopting agentic AI tools faster than smaller companies, with nearly one-third of respondents deciding against purchasing software features because they can now build them internally. Despite higher compute costs, four in five respondents reported improved productivity, though cost savings remain unchanged from the prior year and ROI metrics remain unclear.",
        "execSummary":  "The build-vs-buy calculus in enterprise software is shifting materially: agentic AI tools are now capable enough that large organizations are skipping vendor purchases and developing functionality in-house ΓÇö a dynamic that will compress software vendor revenues and force ISVs to compete on integration depth rather than feature breadth. For enterprise leaders, the productivity signal is strong (80% report improvement) but the cost story is not yet closing, with compute expenses rising and savings flat. This gap between productivity gains and cost savings is the central ROI problem of 2026 agentic deployments. Leaders should set explicit cost-per-outcome targets for agent programs before scaling, not after.",
